# Verify-then-Converge — the loop we run to keep changes on track

**The primitive:** *generate claims → give each a verdict against an authoritative source →
record the evidence → converge on "only justified residue."* Code review (an AI reviewer's
CLI or PR bot) is the most common instance; the same discipline governs a "gate is green"
claim, a subagent's "all fixed", the issue's own requirements, and your own "this is fine".
The reviewer is a **hypothesis generator, not an oracle** — its recall is trustworthy only
inside the diff-visible, single-subsystem, statically-analyzable slice. Outside it, *you*
supply recall: front-load adversarially and drive the real thing.

Depth lives in two documents under `.context/patterns/`:
[`verify-then-converge-core.md`](../../.context/patterns/verify-then-converge-core.md)
(the org doctrine — kit-managed, upgraded by `ai-workflow-scaffold`) and
[`verify-then-converge-playbook.md`](../../.context/patterns/verify-then-converge-playbook.md)
(THIS repo's specifics — its adjudication heuristics, stack taxonomy, reviewer policy,
incidents). The pasted-comment triage flow is the overlay:
[`code-review.mdc`](code-review.mdc).

## When this fires

After completing **substantial** work — a feature, anything security- or contract-shaped, a
dependency upgrade, a removal; committed, or complete-but-uncommitted when commits aren't
authorized (read-only mode) — and **before** opening or updating the PR. Not for one-line
copy or lint tweaks. When the user pastes reviewer comments or says "attend the review",
this loop (with [`code-review.mdc`](code-review.mdc) as the triage format) is the process —
never freestyle it from memory.

## The loop

1. **Commit first — under the user's explicit ask.** The loop runs on the user's request,
   and that ask covers its work and per-pass commits; **absent it, run read-only**: review
   the working tree as it stands and record each pass per the core doctrine's read-only
   record, committing nothing. When committing: branch `<type>/<slug>` off
   **`main`**, never the trunk; branch-guard before every commit, stage explicit
   paths (never `git add -A`), and make every writing git command assert its branch AND
   directory in the same chained invocation. Warm the signing/push credentials up front,
   while the human is present.
2. **Preflight the reviewer.** Consent: standing = the reviewer's config committed on the
   **base branch**, or the review App demonstrably reviewing every PR; otherwise ask once.
   Consent and *activation* are orthogonal — read the config, don't just detect it.
   Secrets-scan the payload the reviewer will actually receive.
   No hay config de CodeRabbit/Copilot Reviewer commiteada ni App de review en el repo. El pass por defecto es el loop in-house (esta regla + [code-review.mdc](code-review.mdc)). Un bot externo necesita decisión explícita del equipo.
3. **Scope to the unreviewed range**, never the whole branch — find prior review-pass
   commits first; do not re-adjudicate settled findings.
4. **Verify every finding against ground truth, then verdict** — **Apply / Modify / Skip /
   Refute / Escalate**, one evidence line each. Prefer executing something over reading
   harder. Severity orders attention; it never assigns trust. A skip rationale is a claim
   and gets the same standard as a finding.
5. **Fix minimally; fix the class, not the instance** — enumerate every site of the class,
   in every path, before declaring it closed.
6. **Validate honestly** — run this repo's gate, know what it does NOT check, and cover
   that too. Behavioral bugs get a regression test that provably fails on revert.
   Gate: `npm run build` (Vite). **No hay tests.** El build no cubre Supabase, RLS, ni flujos de UI. Verificar a mano `dev` + persistencia si el cambio toca órdenes.
7. **Record every pass.** When commits are authorized (step 1): one commit per pass — the
   full verdict tally in the subject (fixed incl. modified / skipped / refuted / escalated),
   one evidence line per non-Apply verdict in the body, a `Review-Pass:` trailer; zero-fix
   passes get `--allow-empty`. In **read-only mode**: no commit, no trailer — write the core
   doctrine's read-only record instead (content fingerprint, the same full tally, the same
   evidence lines). Either way, **never bump the version in a review pass** — releasing is a
   separate, developer-triggered ritual.
8. **Converge by finding class, not count.** Stop at trivial residue or evidence-backed
   skips; the PR bot is the free confirming pass; **never self-merge**, and never commit
   after the bot's approval.

## Non-negotiables

- **Exit codes lie through pipes** — capture the reviewer's `$?` before any filter touches
  the stream; output missing the tool's own scope header is a FAILURE, never "clean".
- **The confirming pass audits your previous pass, not the reviewer** — the apply commit is
  unreviewed code written under momentum.
- **"Cosmetic", "out-of-scope", and "pre-existing" are the three dangerous skip words** —
  the third has a test: did this PR materially change the code hosting the finding?
- **Relaxing a gate or invariant → grep every downstream consumer** of what it guaranteed.
- **OpenSpec/spec artifacts are review surface** — adjudicate on "implementable + preserves
  the artifact's purpose", and the issue itself is also a hypothesis.
- **No PII/secret literals in planning artifacts** — describe the smell, never quote it.
- After a loop that touched docs, emit the `## Context-Update Assessment`
  ([`context-maintenance.mdc`](context-maintenance.mdc)) — never silently update the hubs.
